# Cloudflare Pages headers.
#
# WHY. On 27 August a CSS change went out and did not appear: browsers were
# holding a PARTIAL copy, and a Cloudflare purge did not clear it. The fix
# was fingerprinting the filename, which works — a change is a new URL no
# cache has seen. But it happened again on 30 August, launch day, on the dev
# Mac: Safari held a bad copy of a CURRENT hash and served an unstyled page.
# Chrome and the phone were fine, so it was local browser state, but a
# visitor arriving mid-deploy could hit the same window.
#
# `immutable` closes it. It tells the browser this URL's contents will never
# change, so do not revalidate, ever. Revalidation is the step that goes
# wrong. Safe here precisely because the filename carries the content hash:
# if the file changes, the URL changes, and a year-long max-age can never
# serve anything stale.
#
# ONLY for content-addressed files. Never put immutable on a URL whose
# contents can change under it — index.html most of all.

/assets/css/site.*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/assets/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
